WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.068 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You've been here a bunch,
but it's been a very long gap.

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:03.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
You were last here
for the finale of

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Parks and Recreation."
-That's right.

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:05.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then that's when
our feud started.

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:06.773 line:92%
-That's when our feud started.

00:00:06.773 --> 00:00:09.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And I'm glad we made up.
It's good to be back.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:10.377 size:75% position:38% line:92%
-We finally made up.

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:13.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
We always said
that if a pandemic hit,

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:16.883 align:start size:94% position:6%
we would put
our differences aside.

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:18.084 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:19.252 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-So, I've known you --

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:22.522 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I received your owl.
-You received --

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.824 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm so glad
because you train an owl,

00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:25.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
you train an owl,
you train an owl,

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:28.595
and then the time comes that you
actually have to send him ou

00:00:28.595 --> 00:00:30.263 align:start size:94% position:6%
with a message
wrapped to his leg,

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:31.097 line:92%
and you just hope he gets there.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.867
-Your summons was successful
-Well, I'm glad you're here.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.202 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I know you --
We were talking backstage.

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:39.439 align:start size:94% position:6%
A lot of times,
people will almost confuse you

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:44.244
with Ron Swanson because you are
in life a very crafty gentleman.

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
You are a woodworker by trade.
-Yeah.

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:47.714 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I remember
when I first met you,

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:50.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
you were in Red Hook, Brooklyn
making a canoe.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.385 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's right.
-This is a real thing.

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:52.952 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
So it made sense.

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:57.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
In this show, you play
a quantum-computing expert

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.224 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-That's right.

00:00:58.224 --> 00:00:59.859 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That have any of
the same overlap for you?

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:03.763 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I have not -- You know,
I was down at the RadioShack

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:04.664 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and, uh...

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.333 line:92%
No, no one has accused me of -

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:10.603 size:81% position:41%
No one has confused me
with a genius as yet.

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:12.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Very believable genius
in the show.

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:16.476 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you.
I'm classically trained.

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:18.812 align:start size:94% position:6%
Alex Garland,
our writer/director...

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:20.480 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Who's a genius.
-...is just astonishing.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.616 align:start size:94% position:6%
-His -- I mean,
he wrote a great character

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:25.618 line:92%
And, so, the trick to playing --

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:29.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, like,
if I was cast as a NBA player,

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:32.992 size:88% position:44%
the trick is not to try
and look good dribbling.

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:34.727 size:78% position:39% line:92%
It's just to be cool.

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:35.762 size:75% position:38% line:92%
And people are like,

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:37.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh, clearly,
he can make a free throw.

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:40.900
I mean, look how cool he is.
-Right. So that's the go-to.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.335 line:92%
-Your go-to move is findin

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:43.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
an inherent coolness
to the character.

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:46.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's Acting 101.
-This is a lot of --

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:49.442 size:84% position:42%
Asks a lot of questions
about how technology

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:52.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
affects our lives,
how it affects our free will

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:54.848
Are these questions that you'v
found that you have ever asked

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:57.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
yourselves before
taking on this role?

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.353 line:92%
-Uh, no. I hate technology

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:02.055 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-I hate it.

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:05.625 align:start size:94% position:6%
I grew up in a farming family.
My life is very tangible.

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:10.897 align:start size:94% position:6%
I like to read physical books.
I don't like e-books.

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:13.032 line:92%
I'm reluctantly competent

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:16.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
when it comes to using screens
you know?

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:18.238 line:92%
But I have a system where I --

00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:20.573 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm like, "Okay, I've got to
check these five places.

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:24.177
All right. Nobody needs me
Get that thing out of here

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:27.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now let me get back
to this sandwich."

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-We were both in Chicago.

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:31.985 align:start size:94% position:6%
I feel like I was maybe
a tick after you,

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.521 size:84% position:42%
maybe a half-generation
after you in Chicago.

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:36.556 align:start size:94% position:6%
You had a theater company.
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:38.291 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Defiant Theatre company.
-That's right.

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:41.261
-What were the sort of shows
you guys were doing in Chicago

00:02:41.261 --> 00:02:42.428 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
back in the '90s?

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:44.330 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Defiant was formed
by a group of friends

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.633 align:start size:94% position:6%
that attended
the University of Illinois

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:48.201 line:92%
down in Champaign-Urbana.

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:49.602 size:84% position:42% line:92%
And among other things,

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:51.538 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's a great program
for theater,

00:02:51.538 --> 00:02:53.173 align:start size:94% position:6%
but there was this
special sensei,

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:57.410 size:88% position:44%
a man named Shozo Sato,
who was our true sensei.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.611 line:92%
He taught us Kabuki theater,

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:03.383
which is this amazing Japanese
presentational theater style

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.418 line:92%
And so we would do these shows

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:09.789 align:start size:94% position:6%
that were like a Tex Avery
cartoon, with, like, samurais.

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:13.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
Part of it was probably 'cause
we weren't great at acting

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:16.529
But I was like, "I can do a flip
and crash through a wall.

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:19.432 align:start size:94% position:6%
All right. We can use that
Let's build from it.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:21.134 line:92%
Let's turn that into a story."

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:22.569 size:81% position:41% line:92%
But it was really fun.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:24.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, we had
a great following.

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:27.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we did do good plays,
as well, you know.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.274 size:84% position:42% line:92%
We did realistic stuff.

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:32.145
But my favorite thing were these
sort of carnival-esque shows

00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:34.013 align:start size:94% position:6%
in which we would
often get hurt.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:37.016
-I genuinely think -- and I'
sure you feel the same way -

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:39.852
I count myself so lucky to hav
been in Chicago at that time

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:42.522 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I hope Chicago
is still like it today.

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:44.324 align:start size:94% position:6%
But there were followings
for shows.

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:46.693 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I was
an improviser at the time.

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:48.161 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was great
that you could do a show,

00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:51.030
and I feel like people from al
walks of lives would show up

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.033 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you just learned
how to be a better performer

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.435 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I agree.
I mean, I always say

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:58.271
I think it's the best town
for theater in the country

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:02.041 size:84% position:42%
because it doesn't have
Broadway or Hollywood.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:04.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
It doesn't have
the ulterior motive.

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:07.347 size:81% position:41%
You can't make money
performing in Chicago.

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:10.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
So if you're doing it, it mean
you must really be earnest

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.318 align:start size:94% position:6%
and people recognize that.
-Yeah.

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:14.053 align:start size:94% position:6%
-They come for your work ethic
-Yeah.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:20.326
You -- Your work ethic was s
strong that you also built sets.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:23.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
You did not have a big budget,
I'm assuming, at Defiant.

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:26.332 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No, we did not.
We had a small budget.

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:27.934 line:92%
But we prided ourselves on -

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:31.738 align:start size:94% position:6%
I could drive around the alley
of Chicago for two hours

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:33.673 align:start size:94% position:6%
and collect all the material
I needed

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:37.343
to build any structure
you would want for a set.

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:39.312 line:92%
-Friends of mine from Chicag

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:43.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
who also were fans
of the alleys in Chicago

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:44.617 line:92%
and how you could find things,

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
they said the worst thing
about New York is no alleys.

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.855
-It's true.
-It's just like a no-alley town.

00:04:48.855 --> 00:04:51.624 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. How are you supposed to
furnish an apartment?

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:54.294 size:84% position:42%
-Where were you living,
speaking of apartments,

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:55.328 size:78% position:39% line:92%
in Chicago back then?

00:04:55.328 --> 00:04:59.165
-Well, my main spot, you know --
I bounced around a little 'cause

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:00.967 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was not making
a lot of income.

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:03.936 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I talked
a landlord of a warehouse

00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:06.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
into letting me live
in part of the warehouse.

00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:10.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
This guy was generous.
Because I was like, "Look.

00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:12.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or maybe he thought
I was threatening him.

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:14.747 size:81% position:41%
So, I said, "Listen.
Do you have anybody --

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:16.482 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you have
a night watchman here?"

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:18.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's like, "No."
I'm like, "Somebody might come

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:22.989
in here and mess with your rea
nice warehouse full of stuff

00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:27.093
I would be willing to pay yo
a small amount and live in back,

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:30.396 align:start size:94% position:6%
and maybe I'd build
some scenery in part of this -

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:31.898 line:92%
and have a scenery shop."

00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:33.132 line:92%
And this guy agreed to it.

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:35.001 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I lived in this warehouse
right around the corner

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:37.503 align:start size:94% position:6%
from Steppenwolf
that is now a Crate &amp; Barrel

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:41.474
-Wow. So you obviously had a
eye for real estate back then.

00:05:41.474 --> 00:05:44.410 line:92%
-Yeah. I knew what I was doing

